---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import GlossaryList from '../../../components/GlossaryList.astro';
import { getModule } from '../../../data/modules';
import { includedExperiences, experienceRoute } from '../../../data/experiences';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths';
import Term from '../../../components/Term.astro';
const mod = getModule('rotor-wing');
const contents = [
 ['overview', 'Purpose and learning objectives'], ['blade', 'The blade and its relative wind'],
 ['forces', 'Forces, hover and turns'], ['controls', 'Controls and gyroscopic precession'],
 ['systems', 'Rotor systems, torque and configurations'], ['power', 'Hover, ground effect and power'],
 ['forward', 'Forward flight and dissymmetry of lift'], ['rbs', 'Retreating blade stall and compressibility'],
 ['vrs', 'Vortex ring state'], ['ground', 'Dynamic rollover and ground resonance'],
 ['low-g', 'Low G, mast bumping and low rotor rpm'], ['lte', 'Loss of tail rotor effectiveness'],
 ['autorotation', 'Autorotation'],
 ['terms', 'Terms'], ['check', 'Check your understanding'], ['sources', 'Sources and applicability'],
];
const cite = (n: number) => (pages: string) => `[${n}, ${pages.includes('–') || pages.includes(',') ? 'pp.' : 'p.'} ${pages}]`;
// Military sources first: [1] NAVWEPS 00-80T-80 (printed pages), [2] Army
// FM 3-04.203 (cited by paragraph). Supplementary: [3] the FAA Helicopter
// Flying Handbook, which covers most hazards the military extracts did not.
const n = cite(1), h = cite(3);
const fm = (paras: string) => `[2, ${paras.includes(',') || paras.includes('–') ? '¶¶' : '¶'} ${paras}]`;
const fixed = sitePath('manual/flight-fundamentals/principles-of-flight/');
const airfield = sitePath('manual/airfield-operations/airfield-basics/');
const autorotationPractice = includedExperiences.find((e) => e.slug === 'autorotation');
const practice = autorotationPractice && sitePath(experienceRoute(autorotationPractice));
---
<SiteLayout title={mod.title} activeSection="manual" activeSubject="flight-fundamentals" compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Flight Fundamentals',href:sitePath('manual/flight-fundamentals/')},{label:mod.title}]}>
 <section class="status-panel" data-module-id={mod.id} data-module-revision={mod.revision}>
  <h2>{mod.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {mod.revision} · How a helicopter's rotor flies, written for military pilots of every service: the blade's own relative wind, the forces in a hover and a turn, the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> and pedals, rotor systems and torque, hover power, forward flight and <Term id="Dissymmetry of lift">dissymmetry of lift</Term>, the rotor's hazards and <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term>. It follows the Navy's <em>Aerodynamics for Naval Aviators</em> and the Army's <em>Fundamentals of Flight</em>, with the FAA <em>Helicopter Flying Handbook</em> as the supplementary source for most hazards. Worked numbers use an invented utility helicopter.</p>
  <p>Every figure is interactive: move a control and the drawing and readout follow. The wing itself (lift, drag, <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> and stall) is in the companion module, <a href={fixed}>Fixed-Wing Principles of Flight</a>. Tiltrotors, weight and balance, and type-specific procedures are outside this revision.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <ol>{contents.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
 </nav>

 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>Every US service flies helicopters. The Army's aviation runs from the <Term record="ah-64d">AH-64 Apache</Term> to the <Term id="Tandem rotor">tandem-rotor</Term> <Term record="ch-47f">CH-47 Chinook</Term>; the Marine Corps took the <Term record="ch-47f">Chinook</Term>'s smaller predecessor as the CH-46 Sea Knight; and the <Term record="uh-1h">UH-1 Huey</Term> served all the US armed forces, from Army <Term id="Air assault">air assault</Term> to Air Force special operations and Navy river gunships. Navy helicopters fly plane guard beside the carrier, which is why the Navy's flying-qualities specifications asked for sideways flight up to 30 knots. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('322')}</a></p>
 <p>The physics is the same as a wing's. The Army's <em>Fundamentals of Flight</em> works the same laws through to the rotor: lift varies as the square of the velocity, and past a <Term id="Critical angle of attack">critical angle of attack</Term> the flow over the blade is disrupted and it stalls. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-28, 1-29')}</a> <em>Aerodynamics for Naval Aviators</em> says the same of the helicopter: the rotor is subject to the same physical laws of aerodynamics and motion as the fixed wing, but in much more complicated flow. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('400')}</a> So read the <a href={fixed}>fixed-wing module</a> first for <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>, lift, drag and stall; this module starts where the wing starts to turn.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: explain how rotation and <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term> make each blade's relative wind and <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>; say what the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term>, pedals and throttle each do, and why a <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> input takes effect 90° later; tell semirigid, rigid and <Term id="Fully articulated rotor">fully articulated rotors</Term> apart and say what torque and <Term id="Translating tendency">translating tendency</Term> are; explain why a hover takes the most power and what density altitude, weight and wind do to it; describe <Term id="Translational lift">translational lift</Term>, <Term id="Effective translational lift (ETL)">ETL</Term>, transverse flow and <Term id="Dissymmetry of lift">dissymmetry of lift</Term>; recognise <Term id="Retreating blade stall">retreating blade stall</Term>, compressibility, <Term id="Vortex ring state">vortex ring state</Term>, <Term id="Dynamic rollover">dynamic rollover</Term>, <Term id="Ground resonance">ground resonance</Term>, low G, low rotor rpm and <Term id="Loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE)">loss of tail rotor effectiveness</Term>, and give the first recovery action for each; and explain the regions of an autorotating rotor and why the <Term id="Height/velocity diagram">height–velocity diagram</Term> has areas to avoid.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a conceptual reference, not a flight manual. The example utility helicopter (16,000 lb, a four-bladed 27 ft radius rotor at 260 rpm, 2,000 shp) is invented to put numbers on the ideas. Its rotor turns counterclockwise seen from above, like most American-built single-rotor helicopters, <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-78')}</a> so the advancing blade is on the right; for a clockwise rotor, swap left and right. Your helicopter's speeds, limits and emergency procedures come from its flight manual: the operator's manual for the Army, NATOPS for the Navy and Marine Corps, the -1 for the Air Force.</p>

 <h2 id="blade">The blade and its relative wind</h2>
 <p>A rotor blade is an airfoil, and most light helicopters use symmetrical airfoils for their main rotor blades. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-8')}</a> Its relative wind has two parts. The <strong>rotational relative wind</strong> comes from the blade's own turning: it flows parallel to the plane of rotation and is fastest at the tip, falling uniformly to zero at the mast. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-9')}</a> <em>Aerodynamics for Naval Aviators</em> divides the blade into elements and adds a second part, the <Term>induced flow</Term> or downwash the rotor itself drives down through the disk; the resultant of the two is the relative wind each element meets. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('400–401')}</a> Because the tips move faster than the roots, blades are twisted, with more pitch at the root and less near the tip, to spread the lift more evenly. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-9')}</a></p>
 <p>Two angles follow. The <strong><Term>angle of incidence</Term></strong>, usually called blade pitch, is the mechanical angle between the <Term id="Chord line">chord line</Term> and the plane of rotation, set by the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> and <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term>. The <strong><Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term></strong> is the aerodynamic angle between the <Term id="Chord line">chord line</Term> and the resultant relative wind; <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term>, climb, descent and airspeed all change it with no change in pitch. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-8, 2-12–2-13')}</a> Increase <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> past the critical angle and the blade stalls, as a wing does. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-13')}</a></p>
 <p>The rotor turns at a nearly constant rpm. So, unlike an airplane, which holds an efficient <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> by flying at different airspeeds, the helicopter changes blade angle to cope with altitude and weight: rotor thrust is proportional to air density, a thrust coefficient and the square of the tip speed. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('400')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="blade" aria-labelledby="blade-title">
