   <label for="blade-pitch">Pitch at 75% radius <output for="pitch"></output><input id="blade-pitch" name="pitch" type="range" min="0" max="16" step="0.5" value="8" /></label>
   <label for="blade-induced">Induced flow <output for="induced"></output><input id="blade-induced" name="induced" type="range" min="0" max="60" step="1" value="38" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 380" role="img" aria-label="Rotor from above with the chosen blade station, and that blade section end-on with its rotational relative wind, induced flow, resultant relative wind and angle of attack"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. At 75% of the example rotor's radius the blade meets the air at 327 kt from its rotation. With 38 ft/s of <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term> the relative wind tilts 3.9° downward, so 8° of pitch gives about 4.1° <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Rotational speed Ω r; inflow angle φ = arctan(<Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term> ÷ Ω r); <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> = local pitch − φ, with pitch quoted at 75% radius and an invented 8° twist from root to tip. <Term id="Induced flow">Induced flow</Term> is taken as even across the disk, the simplest momentum-theory assumption; 38 ft/s is the example helicopter's ideal hover value, √(T ÷ 2ρA). The critical angle is about 15°. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-15')}</a></p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="forces">Forces, hover and turns</h2>
 <p>Once it leaves the ground a helicopter is acted on by the same four forces as an airplane: lift, weight, thrust and drag. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-2–2-3')}</a> In powered flight the rotor's total lift-thrust force acts perpendicular to the rotor disk; tilt the disk and the force tilts with it. In a no-wind hover the forces balance and the helicopter stays put; tilt the disk forward and the force divides into lift acting upward and thrust acting in the direction of flight. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-14, 2-17')}</a></p>
 <p>The rotor's drag comes in three kinds. <strong>Profile drag</strong> is the blades' own friction and form drag and changes little with <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>; <strong><Term id="Induced drag">induced drag</Term></strong> comes from the lift itself and is the major cause of drag at low airspeed; <strong><Term id="Parasite drag">parasite drag</Term></strong> comes from the fuselage, mast, gear and other non-lifting parts and grows with the square of the speed. The bottom of the total drag curve is L/D<sub>max</sub>. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-6–2-7')}</a></p>
 <p>A banked turn tilts the disk sideways as well, so only part of the rotor's force holds the helicopter up; to hold altitude and airspeed in the turn, <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> pitch must be increased. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-24–2-25')}</a> The load on the rotor rises as it does on a wing: little up to 30° of bank, then fast, twice the weight at 60°. Each blade carries its share, so a three-bladed rotor's blades each carry a third of it. Turbulence adds sudden load too, because a vertical gust raises the blades' <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>. If the extra power is not there, the helicopter descends or slows. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-5–2-6')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Rotor load in a level turn by bank angle" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Level, coordinated turn: rotor load = weight ÷ cos(bank), shown for the example 16,000 lb helicopter</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Bank</th><th scope="col">Load factor</th><th scope="col">Rotor must carry</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">15°</th><td>1.04 G</td><td>16,565 lb</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">30°</th><td>1.15 G</td><td>18,475 lb</td></tr>
