 </table></div>

 <h2 id="controls">Controls and gyroscopic precession</h2>
 <p>A helicopter pilot has three major controls, the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> and the <Term id="Antitorque pedals">antitorque pedals</Term>, plus a throttle, usually on the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('3-1')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>Collective</Term></strong>, by the pilot's left side: raising it increases the pitch of all the main rotor blades at once and by the same amount, and so the rotor's thrust. More pitch means more drag and less rotor rpm unless power is added, which a governor or correlator does on most turbine helicopters. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('3-2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Cyclic</Term></strong>: it changes each blade's pitch as it goes round, so the disk tilts in the direction the stick moves, and the thrust with it. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('3-3–3-4')}</a> The Navy text compares the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> to an airplane's control stick and the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> to its throttle. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('321')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Antitorque pedals</Term></strong>: they set the pitch, and so the thrust, of the tail rotor. In a hover they control heading; above <Term id="Translational lift">translational lift</Term> they trim the helicopter for coordinated flight while the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> turns it. In tandem, coaxial and intermeshing helicopters the pedals work through the main rotors instead. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('3-4–3-5')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>In a hover every control affects the others: the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> holds position, the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> holds height, the pedals hold heading and the throttle, if no governor does it, holds rpm, and a change to one needs a correction of the other two. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-14')}</a></p>
 <p>The spinning rotor behaves like a gyroscope. A force on it shows its result about 90° later in the direction of rotation: <Term>gyroscopic precession</Term>. So the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> linkage changes a blade's pitch about 90° before the point where the disk should tilt: forward <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term>, on a counterclockwise rotor, takes pitch off the blades on the right side and adds it on the left, and the disk tips forward. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-17, 3-4')}</a> <em>Aerodynamics for Naval Aviators</em> describes the same rig and adds that the rotor's tilt, its thrust and the height of the hub above the centre of gravity set the control moment, so control power falls when rotor thrust is low. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('320–321')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="cyclic" aria-labelledby="cyclic-title">
