 </section>

 <h2 id="systems">Rotor systems, torque and configurations</h2>
 <p>As the rotor turns, centrifugal force pulls the blades straight out from the hub and gives them their rigidity; lift bends them up, so in flight they form a shallow cone: <Term>coning</Term>. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-77')}</a> <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-16')}</a> Excessive <Term id="Coning">coning</Term> comes from low rotor rpm or high gross weight; it stresses the components and costs lift, <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-77')}</a> and if rpm falls far enough the blades can fold up with no chance of recovery. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-16')}</a> As blades flap and cone their centres of mass move in and out, so, like a skater drawing in her arms, they speed up and slow down within each turn. Rotor systems are built around these motions. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-16–2-17, 4-5')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>Semirigid rotor</Term></strong>: usually two blades rigidly fixed to a hub that teeters on the mast, so they flap as a unit, one up while the other goes down; they feather but have no lead-lag hinge. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('4-2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Fully articulated rotor</Term></strong>: each blade can flap, lead and lag, and feather on its own; found on helicopters with more than two blades. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('4-4–4-5')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Rigid rotor</Term></strong>: no <Term id="Flapping">flapping</Term> or lead-lag hinges; the blades bend to allow those motions and are feathered at the hub. It is very responsive and not usually susceptible to <Term id="Mast bumping">mast bumping</Term>, but passes more vibration into the cabin. Bearingless rotors are a variation that relies on the hub and blade structure to absorb the stresses. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('4-3–4-4')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Whatever the design, the Navy's text points out, the rotor corrects for forward flight the same way aerodynamically, so what is said about rotor aerodynamics holds for every rotor system: Juan de la Cierva's <Term id="Flapping">flapping</Term> hinges did it on the autogiro, the see-saw rotor does it by rocking the hub and the <Term id="Rigid rotor">rigid rotor</Term> by <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> pitch change. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('402')}</a></p>
 <h3>Torque and the tail rotor</h3>
 <p>Driving the rotor twists the fuselage the other way. Torque must be counteracted, and in a single-rotor helicopter the antitorque (tail) rotor does it; give it less pedal than needed and the nose turns in the direction of torque. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-78, 1-79')}</a> The tail rotor is itself a rotor and takes engine power, and its greatest demands come when the engine is busiest: the most critical condition is a hover at maximum gross weight. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('321–322')}</a> Its thrust also pushes a hovering helicopter sideways, in the direction of tail rotor thrust: <Term>translating tendency</Term>. Designers counter it by tilting the mast, rigging the controls so the disk sits slightly tilted, or accepting that the helicopter hangs one skid low. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-15')}</a> Hung from one point, the fuselage also swings like a pendulum, which over-controlling makes worse. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-15–2-16')}</a></p>
 <h3>Configurations</h3>
 <p>Most helicopters have a single main rotor and a tail rotor, the layout Igor Sikorsky settled on. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('1-4')}</a> A <Term>tandem rotor</Term> helicopter such as the <Term record="ch-47f">Chinook</Term> has two main rotors turning in opposite directions, so their torques cancel, no tail rotor is needed and all the engines' power can go into lift. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('1-4, 4-6')}</a> It yaws by tilting its rotors opposite ways with differential <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term>, and pitches by tilting both or by differential <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, more thrust on one rotor and less on the other. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('321–323')}</a> A <Term>coaxial rotor</Term> stacks two counter-rotating rotors on one mast, like Kamov's <Term record="ka-50">Ka-50</Term>; an intermeshing rotor sets two masts at a slight angle so the blades mesh, as on the Air Force's HH-43 Huskie firefighting helicopter. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('1-4–1-5, 4-6')}</a> Other antitorque designs are the Fenestron (a fan in the tail) and the NOTAR. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('1-3')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="power">Hover, ground effect and power</h2>
 <p>To hover, the rotor drives a large mass of air downward. That <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term> is the primary cause of the high power a hover needs, and the tip vortices, part of the <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term>, add <Term id="Induced drag">induced drag</Term>. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-88, 1-89')}</a> The Navy text puts it as momentum: the rotor makes lift by accelerating air downward, as a jet engine makes thrust by accelerating air out of its tailpipe. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('400')}</a> It is the air that counts, not the ground: hovering in a 20 knot wind is aerodynamically the same as flying at 20 knots in still air. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('400')}</a></p>
 <p>Near the surface, <Term>ground effect</Term> reduces the downward velocity of the <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term>, so the same lift takes less pitch and less power. It reaches to about one rotor diameter, and is strongest over smooth, hard surfaces and weaker over tall grass, rough terrain and water. Out of <Term id="Ground effect">ground effect</Term> the <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term> is stronger, and hovering takes more pitch and more power. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-12–2-13')}</a> More power is required in the hover than in any other flight regime, so if a helicopter can hover it can usually take off. As density altitude rises, more power is needed to hover until the power required equals the power available: the hover ceiling, which falls as weight rises. The in-ground-effect ceiling is usually higher than the out-of-ground-effect one, and the FAA advises planning an out-of-ground-effect hover wherever the landing area is uncertain. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('7-4–7-5')}</a> Density altitude, weight and wind are the three major factors; a headwind gives <Term id="Translational lift">translational lift</Term> and cuts the power needed, while crosswinds and tailwinds take tail rotor thrust, and so power, away from the main rotor. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('7-2')}</a></p>
 <p>Once the helicopter moves, <Term id="Translational lift">translational lift</Term> reduces the power it needs; power required falls to a minimum at the best rate-of-climb speed and then rises again as <Term id="Parasite drag">parasite drag</Term> takes over, until it meets the power available at the top level speed. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-17, 2-19')}</a> Figure 3 builds that curve for the example helicopter.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="power" aria-labelledby="power-title">
  <h3 id="power-title" class="fig-title">Figure 3 — Power required and power available</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="power-speed">True airspeed <output for="speed"></output><input id="power-speed" name="speed" type="range" min="0" max="180" step="5" value="0" /></label>
   <label for="power-weight">Gross weight <output for="weight"></output><input id="power-weight" name="weight" type="range" min="12000" max="18000" step="500" value="16000" /></label>
   <label for="power-alt">Density altitude <output for="alt"></output><input id="power-alt" name="alt" type="range" min="0" max="10000" step="500" value="0" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 3, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 400" role="img" aria-label="Power required against airspeed with its induced, profile and parasite parts, the power available line, the minimum-power speed and the top level speed"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. At 16,000 lb at sea level the example helicopter needs 1,688 shp to hover out of <Term id="Ground effect">ground effect</Term>, against 2,000 available. Power required falls to 957 shp at 72 kt and rises again to meet the power available at about 164 kt. At 8,000 ft density altitude the engine gives only 1,572 shp, less than the 1,765 shp the hover needs: the helicopter can fly but not hover out of <Term id="Ground effect">ground effect</Term>.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Invented helicopter: 27 ft radius, 260 rpm, solidity 0.083, blade profile drag coefficient 0.010, 25 ft² of equivalent parasite area. Induced power = 1.15 T v<sub>i</sub>, with v<sub>i</sub> from momentum theory (√(T ÷ 2ρA) in the hover, falling with speed); profile power = (σ C<sub>d0</sub> ÷ 8) ρ A (Ω R)³ (1 + 4.65 μ²); parasite power = ½ ρ f V³. Power available falls in proportion to density (illustrative). Out of <Term id="Ground effect">ground effect</Term>, still air; the speed limit set by <Term id="Retreating blade stall">retreating blade stall</Term> is not modelled.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="forward">Forward flight and dissymmetry of lift</h2>
 <p>As the helicopter gains speed, or meets a headwind, the rotor flies into air it has not already disturbed. <Term id="Induced flow">Induced flow</Term> falls and the same blade pitch gives more lift: <Term>translational lift</Term>. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-24')}</a> <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-21')}</a> Between about 16 and 24 knots the disk completely outruns the recirculation of its old vortices: <Term id="Effective translational lift (ETL)">effective translational lift</Term> (<Term id="Effective translational lift (ETL)">ETL</Term>), felt as a transient vibration and a jump in performance. The tail rotor gains efficiency the same way, <strong>translational thrust</strong>, which yaws the nose left on a counterclockwise rotor until the pilot eases the left pedal. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-22–2-23')}</a> On the way through, the front of the disk loses <Term id="Induced flow">induced flow</Term> sooner than the back, and the difference, <Term>transverse flow effect</Term>, shows as vibration around 12 to 15 knots and a slight roll to the right through about 20 knots; the vibration is easily mistaken for <Term id="Effective translational lift (ETL)">ETL</Term>. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-23')}</a></p>
 <p>In forward flight the advancing blade meets the air at its rotational speed plus the helicopter's airspeed, the retreating blade at its rotational speed minus it. Left alone, the difference, <Term>dissymmetry of lift</Term>, would roll the helicopter toward the retreating side; the Navy text notes that it defeated many early helicopter and autogiro projects. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-19–2-20')}</a> <a href="#source-navweps">{n('402')}</a> <Term>Flapping</Term> is the primary means of compensating for it: the advancing blade flaps up, which reduces its <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>, and the retreating blade flaps down, which increases it; the pitch change the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> makes around the disk also creates differential lift. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-32, 1-33')}</a> <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-20')}</a> The same <Term id="Flapping">flapping</Term> tilts the disk back as speed builds, so the nose rises: <Term>blowback</Term>, which the pilot holds off with forward <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> until the transition is complete. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-20')}</a></p>
