 </section>
 <p>Look along the blades at high speed and the angles of attack vary widely. The whole advancing blade makes positive lift. On the retreating side, next to the hub, the air comes from the trailing edge (reverse flow); further out is a region of negative stall, then negative lift, then positive lift, and near the tip the <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> climbs past the stall. <Term id="Flapping">Flapping</Term> and <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> even out the lift on the two sides but not the drag, and the varying drag shakes the rotor and adds to the helicopter's vibration. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('402–403')}</a></p>
 <p>The rotor also makes the helicopter hard to fly hands-off. In forward flight a rotor is unstable with <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>, because a nose-up disturbance raises the advancing blade's lift more than the retreating blade's and the gyroscopic rotor tips further back; it does have speed stability, which gives a hover some apparent stability. Most helicopters without artificial stabilization have an unstable long-period (phugoid) oscillation, which is why helicopters meant for instrument flight need stabilization. In the Navy text's words, flying qualities that would be unsatisfactory in a fixed-wing aircraft are normal for helicopters. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('319, 323–324')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="rbs">Retreating blade stall and compressibility</h2>
 <p>To make the same lift as the advancing blade, the retreating blade must work at a higher <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>, and the faster the helicopter flies the higher it goes. At high forward speed the tip of the retreating blade passes its stall angle, and the stall spreads inboard from the tip as speed or blade pitch rises. <Term>Retreating blade stall</Term> becomes severe, and control marginal, when the outer quarter of the retreating blade is stalled; with about 15 percent of the disk stalled, control is impossible. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('402–404')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Signs:</strong> rotor roughness, erratic stick forces, and a vibration and stick shake at a frequency set by the number of blades (three beats per revolution on a three-bladed rotor), then a pitch-up and loss of control. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('404')}</a> The FAA lists a low-frequency vibration, the nose pitching up and a roll toward the retreating side. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-11')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>What brings it on:</strong> high airspeed, low rotor rpm, high gross weight, high density altitude, high <Term id="Load factor">load factor</Term>, turbulence and abrupt control movements, all of which raise the retreating blade's <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>. Because a blade needs more angle at altitude, <Term id="Retreating blade stall">retreating blade stall</Term> comes at a lower airspeed the higher you fly, and manufacturers publish a never-exceed speed (V<sub>NE</sub>) that falls with altitude. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('404')}</a> <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-11')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Recovery:</strong> reduce the blade <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> below the stall, by lowering <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, slowing down, increasing rotor rpm and easing the maneuver. Forward <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> to hold the nose down only makes it worse, because it raises the retreating blade's <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term>. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('404')}</a> The FAA adds that aft <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> worsens it too, that the first step is usually to reduce <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, and that the cure follows the cause: roll out of the steep turn that brought it on, or restore low rpm. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-11')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Warning is good when the stall is approached slowly; it is poor only when blade pitch is raised quickly, so a severe stall is most likely in abrupt control movements or rapid accelerated maneuvers. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('404')}</a></p>
 <p>The advancing tip has the opposite problem. It meets the highest relative speed, and when its <Term id="Mach number">Mach number</Term> passes the blade section's critical <Term id="Mach number">Mach number</Term>, drag rises sharply: more power is needed to hold rotor rpm, and the rotor runs rough, shakes the stick and twists its blades. High airspeed, high rotor rpm, high weight, high density altitude, low temperature and turbulence bring it on. <Term id="Retreating blade stall">Retreating blade stall</Term> and compressibility share most of their causes and their recovery, but compressibility comes at <em>high</em> rpm and <Term id="Retreating blade stall">retreating blade stall</Term> at <em>low</em> rpm, so the rpm correction is opposite. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('404–405')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="vrs">Vortex ring state</h2>
 <p>In an out-of-ground-effect hover some air curls up around the blade tips and back in from above: tip vortices. Let the helicopter settle into its own downwash and the vortices grow, the inner blades meet air coming up through the disk, and much of the engine's power goes into stirring a doughnut of air: <Term>vortex ring state</Term>, formerly called settling with power. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-9')}</a> The Navy text explains it the same way: upward flow in the centre of the disk and downward flow outside it, the main lifting part of the rotor not stalled, and a net thrust that can fall toward zero. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('408–410')}</a></p>
 <p>The FAA gives the combination of conditions likely to cause it in any helicopter: a vertical or nearly vertical descent of at least 300 feet per minute, the rotor using some of the available power (20 to 100 percent), and a horizontal speed slower than <Term id="Effective translational lift (ETL)">ETL</Term>. Steep approaches with a tailwind and out-of-ground-effect hovers without precise height control lead into it, and it can follow a quick stop or the recovery from an <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term>. A fully developed <Term id="Vortex ring state">vortex ring state</Term> brings uncommanded pitch and roll, little or no <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> authority, and a descent that may approach 6,000 feet per minute. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-9–11-10')}</a></p>
