 </section>
 <p>Recovery means leaving the vortex. The pilot's instinct, more <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, only enlarges the stalled area and increases the descent. The traditional recovery is to increase airspeed with forward <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> and/or partially lower the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, until the helicopter passes through <Term id="Effective translational lift (ETL)">ETL</Term> into a normal climb. The Vuichard recovery instead uses lateral <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> with more power and antitorque thrust to stop the descent; in most helicopters it is the quickest exit. Training entries should allow recovery by at least 1,000 feet above the ground. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-10')}</a> The 1965 Navy text also recommended diving out as the recovery that loses least altitude, and noted that many cases called "power settling" are really a high sink rate with too little power to stop an approach, common when heavy or at high density altitude. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('410–411')}</a> Follow your flight manual's procedure.</p>

 <h2 id="ground">Dynamic rollover and ground resonance</h2>
 <p>A helicopter with one skid or wheel on the ground can roll over it. <Term>Dynamic rollover</Term> needs three things: a rolling moment, a pivot point other than the normal centre of gravity, and rotor thrust greater than weight. The pivot can be a skid caught on a tie-down, an object, ice, mud or soft asphalt, or a slope. Past the critical rollover angle, about 5 to 8°, the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> no longer has the range to stop the roll and thrust rolls the helicopter onto its side. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-11–11-12')}</a> Quickly reducing <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> is the most effective way to stop it, because it takes the thrust away; a smooth, moderate reduction, about full up to full down in two seconds, may be enough, while dumping the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> can let a blade strike the fuselage or bounce the helicopter over the other way. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-12–11-13')}</a> With a counterclockwise rotor it is most critical with the right skid down, a crosswind from the left and a right lateral centre of gravity. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-12')}</a> Slopes are covered in the <a href={airfield}>Airfield Operations module</a>'s helicopter material and your flight manual.</p>
 <p><Term>Ground resonance</Term> affects helicopters with articulated rotors on the ground. A hard landing on one wheel can shove the blades out of their even spacing on their drag hinges; the out-of-balance rotor then shakes the airframe at its natural frequency, and with the engine feeding it the vibration can destroy the helicopter in seconds. If rotor rpm is low, close the throttle and lower the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> fully; if rpm is in the normal range, lift off and let the blades rephase before landing again. Rigid and <Term id="Semirigid rotor">semirigid rotors</Term>, with no drag hinge, do not suffer it, and skid gear is less prone than wheels. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-11')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="low-g">Low G, mast bumping and low rotor rpm</h2>
 <p>Helicopters rely on positive G for much or all of their control response, because the <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> controls the fuselage by tilting the rotor's thrust. In low G, such as a pushover from moderate or high speed, thrust and control authority fall away. A rotor with three or more blades and offset hinges keeps some control; a two-bladed teetering rotor keeps none. If the fuselage then rolls and the pilot answers with opposite <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term>, the angle between disk and fuselage can exceed the hub's clearance and the hub strikes the mast: <Term>mast bumping</Term>, which can sever the mast. The FAA notes it was first met in military nap-of-the-earth flying, and has caused numerous military and civilian fatal accidents. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-14–11-15')}</a> Recover by first gently applying aft <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> to restore normal G, then correct the roll; in two-bladed helicopters avoid abrupt forward <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> and descend with the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> instead, and slow down in turbulence. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-14–11-15')}</a> <Term id="Flapping">Flapping</Term> angles, and so the risk, grow with high speed, low rotor rpm, high density altitude, high weight and turbulence. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('4-3')}</a></p>
 <p>A helicopter needs rotor rpm as an airplane needs airspeed. Let rpm fall below the safe range and the blades must fly at ever higher angles of attack; at the critical angle, about 15°, the whole <Term id="Rotor stall">rotor stalls</Term>. <Term>Rotor stall</Term> is not <Term id="Retreating blade stall">retreating blade stall</Term>: it can happen at any airspeed, the rotor stops carrying the helicopter, the blades can blow back into the tail boom, and it is not recoverable; above about 50 feet it is likely to be fatal. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-15–11-16')}</a> Low rpm comes from not lowering the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> at once after an engine failure, or, more often, from demanding more power than the engine has, overpitching, at high density altitude or heavy weight. A 10 percent loss of rpm costs about 10 percent of the engine's power, so it feeds on itself. The signs are the rpm gauge and warning, a yaw, less engine noise and, at speed or in turns, more vibration. Recover at once: lower the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, increase throttle if available, and use aft <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> to hold a level attitude. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-16')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="lte">Loss of tail rotor effectiveness</h2>
 <p><Term id="Loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE)">Loss of tail rotor effectiveness</Term> (<Term id="Loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE)">LTE</Term>) is an uncommanded, rapid yaw toward the advancing blade, to the right on a counterclockwise rotor, that does not stop by itself. It comes from the airflow around the tail rotor, not a mechanical failure, and can affect any single-rotor helicopter with a tail rotor. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-18')}</a> High power at low speed, out of <Term id="Ground effect">ground effect</Term>, in the wrong wind, asks more of the tail rotor than it may be able to give. The FAA names three wind regions for a counterclockwise rotor: 285 to 315° (left front), where 10 to 30 knot winds blow the main rotor vortex into the tail rotor; 120 to 240° (tailwinds), where the helicopter weathervanes and a right yaw can accelerate; and 210 to 330°, where a tail rotor <Term id="Vortex ring state">vortex ring state</Term> makes the pedals work hard. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-19–11-21')}</a> Avoid low, slow flight out of <Term id="Ground effect">ground effect</Term>, tailwinds below 30 knots, large power changes at low speed and fast right turns; keep maximum power-on rotor rpm. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-20–11-21')}</a> If an unanticipated right yaw starts: full left pedal, forward <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> to gain speed and, if altitude permits, reduce power; if the rotation cannot be stopped near the ground, an <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term> may be the best course. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-21')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="autorotation">Autorotation</h2>
 <p>An engine failure does not stop the rotor. A freewheeling unit lets the rotor turn without the engine, and by lowering the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, which must be done immediately, the pilot cuts lift and drag and starts a descent, so air flows up through the disk and keeps the rotor turning. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-25, 11-2')}</a> The tail rotor is still driven by the main rotor, so the pedals still control heading. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-2')}</a> An <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term> is also the answer to a complete tail rotor failure, since there is virtually no torque in one. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-2')}</a></p>
 <p>Look along an autorotating blade, the Navy text explains, and the forces differ with radius. Near the tip, at low <Term id="AOA">angle of attack</Term> and high speed, the blade's lift and drag leave a net drag in the plane of rotation that slows the rotor; at about half span, the inflow tilts the lift forward enough to give a net forward pull that drives it. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('405–406')}</a> The FAA names three regions in a vertical <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term>: the <strong>driven region</strong>, the outer 30 percent or so; the <strong>driving region</strong>, normally between 25 and 70 percent of the radius; and the <strong>stall region</strong>, the inner 25 percent, which is above its stall angle and adds drag. The <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> sets their balance and so the rpm. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-26')}</a> <Term id="Autorotation">Autorotation</Term> is essentially stable: if the rotor slows, the driving region grows and speeds it up again; if it speeds up, the driven region grows. But let rpm fall too far and the rotor becomes unstable and keeps slowing unless the pilot acts at once. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('407')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="auto" aria-labelledby="auto-title">
  <h3 id="auto-title" class="fig-title">Figure 6 — Blade regions in a vertical autorotation</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="auto-collective">Collective <output for="collective"></output><input id="auto-collective" name="collective" type="range" min="-3" max="3" step="1" value="0" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 6, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 340" role="img" aria-label="Rotor from above and one blade from hub to tip, divided into the stall, driving and driven regions"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Balanced, the inner 25 percent of the blade is stalled, the driving region runs from 25 to 70 percent of the radius and the outer 30 percent is driven, so rotor rpm holds. Raising the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> shrinks the driving region and rpm falls; lowering it does the opposite.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Balanced sizes are the FAA handbook's. How far the boundaries move with each step of <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> is illustrative; the handbook gives the direction, not the amount. In forward flight the regions shift outboard on the retreating side. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-26')}</a></p>
 </section>
 <p>Airspeed and rotor rpm control the rate of descent. It is high at zero airspeed, least at about 50 to 60 knots in a typical helicopter, and rises again beyond that; each type's flight manual gives its own <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term> airspeed and rpm. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-2–11-3')}</a> The Navy text distinguishes the speed for the minimum rate of descent from the faster speed for the greatest glide distance, found where a line from the origin touches the curve of descent rate against airspeed, just as for a gliding airplane. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('407–408')}</a> The FAA puts the maximum-range speed at usually about 25 percent faster than the minimum-descent speed. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('7-4')}</a> Raising the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> lowers rpm and lowering it raises rpm; aft <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term>, in trim, also sends more air up through the disk and raises rpm. Accident investigations found pilots facing a real failure at cruise speed slow to apply down <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>, aft <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> and pedal together. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-3')}</a></p>
 <p>Near the ground the pilot flares with aft <Term id="Cyclic">cyclic</Term> to slow down, which also raises the rpm, and the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> keeps rpm in limits. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-26')}</a> The last cushion comes from the energy stored in the spinning rotor: raising the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term> converts it into a brief burst of lift. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('408–409')}</a> <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-2')}</a> A rotor with little stored energy loses rpm fast after the failure, which leaves the pilot less time to lower the <Term id="Collective">collective</Term>. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('408')}</a></p>
 <h3>The height–velocity diagram</h3>
 <p>Some combinations of height and airspeed leave too little time, height or speed to enter a steady <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term>. The flight manual's <Term id="Height/velocity diagram">height–velocity diagram</Term> shades them. Low and slow, high above a survivable drop but well below <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term> speed, there is not enough height to accelerate; the 1965 Navy text put the minimum at about 300 feet for most small helicopters and 500 to 600 feet for larger ones. Fast and close to the ground, there is too little time to flare before hitting the ground or striking the tail. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('7-2–7-3')}</a> <a href="#source-navweps">{n('409')}</a> The diagram applies only within its gross weight and density altitude, and multiengine helicopters that can fly on one engine do not show the fast, low area. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('7-3–7-4')}</a> The usual safe takeoff stays out of the shaded areas by accelerating close to the ground and climbing as speed approaches a safe <Term id="Autorotation">autorotation</Term> speed. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('7-3')}</a></p>
 {practice && <p>The entry, descent, flare and touchdown are taken step by step in <a href={practice}>Autorotation Practice</a>, which ends in a standalone engine failure exercise.</p>}

