 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>You raise the collective in a hover. What happens to the blades, and what must happen to the power?</summary><p>All the blades take more pitch at once, so thrust rises; so does their drag, so power must rise to hold rotor rpm (a governor or correlator does it on most turbine helicopters).</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why does forward cyclic change blade pitch on the sides of the disk, not at the front and back?</summary><p>The rotor acts like a gyroscope: the flapping a pitch change causes peaks about 90° later in rotation. Least pitch on the right of a counterclockwise rotor makes the blades lowest over the nose.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The same helicopter can hover at sea level but not at 8,000 ft density altitude. Why?</summary><p>Hover takes the most power of any flight regime, it takes more as the air thins, and the engine gives less. Where power required meets power available is the hover ceiling, lower at higher weight.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Accelerating through about 12 to 15 knots you feel a vibration and the helicopter rolls slightly right. Is that ETL?</summary><p>Probably not yet: that is transverse flow effect. ETL comes between about 16 and 24 knots, with its own transient vibration and a jump in performance.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In a helicopter with a counterclockwise rotor, which side of the disk is advancing in forward flight?</summary><p>The right side. Its blades add the forward speed to their own; the left side's blades subtract it.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Flying fast and heavy at altitude, you feel the stick shake, the nose pitches up and the helicopter rolls left. What is it, and what do you do?</summary><p>Retreating blade stall. Reduce blade angle of attack: usually lower the collective first, slow down, restore rotor rpm, ease the maneuver. Don't push the nose down with forward cyclic.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Compressibility and retreating blade stall have similar causes. What is the difference in recovery?</summary><p>Rotor rpm: compressibility comes at high rpm and retreating blade stall at low rpm, so the rpm correction is opposite.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You're descending almost vertically at 800 fpm, below ETL, with power applied. Why is more collective the wrong fix?</summary><p>Those are the conditions for vortex ring state: more collective enlarges the stalled area and the descent. Gain airspeed and/or partially lower collective, or use the Vuichard recovery.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Lifting off, the helicopter starts to roll over a skid stuck in the mud. What stops it?</summary><p>A smooth, prompt reduction of collective, which removes the thrust that is rolling it. Opposite cyclic alone cannot stop dynamic rollover.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In a two-bladed helicopter you push over and feel weightless; the fuselage starts rolling right. What first?</summary><p>Gently apply aft cyclic to restore positive G, then correct the roll. Correcting the roll in low G risks mast bumping.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Hovering low and slow with a left-front wind, the nose starts a fast right yaw that won't stop. What do you do?</summary><p>That is loss of tail rotor effectiveness: full left pedal, forward cyclic to gain speed, and reduce power if altitude permits.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The engine quits in cruise. What is the first control movement, and why so fast?</summary><p>Lower the collective immediately: the high-pitch blades are losing rpm, and low rotor rpm can end in an unrecoverable rotor stall.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In an autorotation the rotor rpm is too high. Which way do you move the collective?</summary><p>Up: raising it adds pitch, grows the driven region and slows the rotor. Lower it again as rpm returns to the normal range.</p></details>

