 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/rotor-wing-evidence.md">Rotor-Wing Principles of Flight evidence</a>. The FAA handbook is cited where the military texts read for this revision do not cover a point: most of the hazards (<Term id="Dynamic rollover">dynamic rollover</Term>, <Term id="Ground resonance">ground resonance</Term>, low G, <Term id="Rotor stall">rotor stall</Term>, <Term id="Loss of tail rotor effectiveness (LTE)">loss of tail rotor effectiveness</Term>), the controls and rotor systems, and the <Term id="Vortex ring state">vortex ring state</Term> conditions and Vuichard recovery, which postdate the 1965 Navy text. Aircraft examples link to the site's encyclopedia, which carries their sources.</p>
 <p>The blade-element, power and rotor-speed figures are standard rotor mechanics (momentum and blade-element theory in their simplest forms) applied to an invented helicopter; the tests check them against hand-worked values. Figures 1–6 are original line drawings, not reproductions of any publication's figure, and are not to scale except where a caption gives the scale.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: rotor-wing principles for military helicopter pilots of every service. The example helicopter's numbers are invented and apply to no real type. Your helicopter's operator's manual, NATOPS or -1 governs its limits and emergency procedures. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
